        {
          "subsection_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_subsection_14",
          "subsection_title": "Common OSCE questions and model answers",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_group_16",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. What is an ABG syringe used for?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It collects arterial blood for measurement of pH, PaCO₂, PaO₂ and other parameters supported by the analyzer."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. Why is heparin used?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Heparin prevents clot formation in the sample. Dedicated dry, balanced heparin helps reduce dilution and electrolyte-related interference."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. Why must air bubbles be removed?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Contact with atmospheric air can alter measured oxygen and carbon dioxide tensions and may affect pH, leading to misleading interpretation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. Why must the sample be analyzed promptly?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Blood cells continue to consume oxygen and produce carbon dioxide and acids after collection, potentially changing the result over time."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. What is the usual sampling site?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The radial artery at the wrist is commonly used because it is accessible and can often be compressed effectively after sampling."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. Name four complications of arterial sampling."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Pain, haematoma, bleeding and arterial spasm. Infection, vascular injury and distal ischaemia are additional possible complications."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. What information should accompany an ABG result?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Patient identification, collection time, oxygen delivery or inspired oxygen concentration, relevant ventilator settings and pertinent clinical information."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q8. What is the difference between PaO₂ and SpO₂?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "PaO₂ is the partial pressure of dissolved oxygen measured from arterial blood. SpO₂ is the estimated percentage of haemoglobin saturated with oxygen, usually measured by pulse oximetry."
                }
              ]
